  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T3",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests downstream QA accuracy across model sizes, a different measure from pretraining loss, and reports both increases and decreases with size.",
    "gate_note": "The study does not measure held-out cross-entropy or training compute. Rows record the study's own QA-accuracy results for model-size comparisons discussed as evidence about scaling. Results cited from other work and standalone model scores not used in a size comparison are not included.",
    "test_reason": "The study compares zero-shot closed-book generative QA accuracy, judged by humans, across sizes within model families (LLaMA, OPT, MPT, WizardLM, Airoboros, Alpaca). This is a new downstream setting with results on whether performance improves with scale (V6). It measures no loss, fits no curves and has no compute-controlled design. T3 fits better than T2.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors find no clear scale tendency: LLaMA and OPT 30B and the 70B Luminous underperform smaller models, though larger models do better in general. They attribute this to undertraining, may be technical multi-GPU problems, and the low-accuracy 'blade' region of hockey-stick curves. They conclude that fine-tuning data matters more than parameter count.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study measures zero-shot closed-book QA accuracy across available models of differing sizes; it does not measure pretraining loss or fit scaling laws.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The authors report that model performance varies substantially with fine-tuning and training data. They also note prompt wording sensitivity for Luminous, but that is not a size comparison.",
    "fits_no_version": [],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Artificial Intelligence and Applications",
    "problems": "Only 110 questions were used. The authors performed human evaluation themselves, and independent verification of results for some larger models is needed; they note possible technical problems when inferencing with multiple GPUs. Models differ in training, fine-tuning, and quantization, so the size comparisons are not controlled scaling experiments.",
    "rows_left_out": "LLaMA 65B is listed as 0% in Table 3, but the text says the checkpoint did not produce usable results after prompt and model attempts; it is not recorded as a valid performance outcome. Other model scores are omitted unless the paper uses them in a size comparison or discusses them as evidence about scale.",
